WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:16.198
Raise your hand, and be honest,

00:00:16.222 --> 00:00:20.135
if you've used the phrase "crazy busy"

00:00:20.159 --> 00:00:25.273
to describe your day,
your week, your month.

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:29.386
I'm an emergency-room doctor,

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.390
and "crazy busy" is a phrase
you will never hear me use.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.459
And after today,

00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:40.340
I hope you'll stop using it, too.

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:45.315
Here's why you cannot afford
to use "crazy"

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:47.328
to describe your busy.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:51.379
Because when we are
in what I refer to as Crazy Busy Mode,

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:56.137
we are simply less capable
of handling the busy.

00:00:56.844 --> 00:00:58.035
Here's what happens.

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:00.336
Your stress hormones rise and stay there,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.069
your executive function
in the prefrontal cortex declines.

00:01:04.093 --> 00:01:07.919
That means your memory, your judgment,
your impulse control deteriorate,

00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:12.306
and the brain areas for anger
and anxiety are activated.

00:01:12.825 --> 00:01:13.975
Do you feel that?

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.211
Here's the thing.

00:01:17.235 --> 00:01:20.887
You can be as busy
as an emergency department

00:01:20.911 --> 00:01:24.005
without feeling like you're crazy busy.

00:01:25.204 --> 00:01:26.362
How?

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.244
By using the same tactics that we use.

00:01:28.268 --> 00:01:32.425
Our brains all process stress
in similar fundamental ways.

00:01:32.449 --> 00:01:34.664
But how we react to it

00:01:34.688 --> 00:01:37.323
has been shown by research
to be modifiable,

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:41.719
whether it's emergencies
or just daily, day-in, day-out stress.

00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.394
Now contrast Crazy Busy Mode

00:01:45.418 --> 00:01:48.552
with how I think of us
in the ER -- Ready Mode.

00:01:49.243 --> 00:01:51.980
Ready Mode means whatever comes in
through those doors,

00:01:52.004 --> 00:01:53.871
whether it's a multiple-car pileup,

00:01:53.895 --> 00:01:57.377
or a patient having chest pain
while stuck in an elevator,

00:01:57.401 --> 00:02:01.609
or another patient with an item
stuck where it shouldn't be.

00:02:02.498 --> 00:02:05.076
When you're know you're dying to ask.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.474
(Laughter)

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:11.403
Even on those days when you would swear
you were being punked,

00:02:11.427 --> 00:02:13.838
we're not afraid of it.

00:02:14.664 --> 00:02:18.474
Because we know that whatever comes in
through those ER double doors,

00:02:18.498 --> 00:02:20.029
that we can handle it.

00:02:20.053 --> 00:02:21.339
That we're ready.

00:02:21.887 --> 00:02:24.011
That's Ready Mode.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.339
We've trained for it,

00:02:26.363 --> 00:02:28.531
and you can, too.

00:02:29.236 --> 00:02:30.386
Here's how.

00:02:30.736 --> 00:02:32.863
Step one to go
from Crazy Mode to Ready Mode

00:02:32.887 --> 00:02:35.506
is to relentlessly triage.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.948
In Crazy Mode, you're always busy,
always stressed,

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:41.840
because you're reacting to every challenge
with the same response.

00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:44.950
Contrast that with Ready Mode,

00:02:44.974 --> 00:02:46.495
where we triage,

00:02:46.519 --> 00:02:50.151
which means we prioritize
by degree of urgency.

00:02:50.763 --> 00:02:53.508
This isn't just a nice way
to get your to-do list done.

00:02:53.532 --> 00:02:55.492
Work by Dr. Robert Sapolsky

00:02:55.516 --> 00:02:59.654
shows that individuals who cannot
differentiate threat from non-threat

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:02.218
and react to everything
with the same response

00:03:02.242 --> 00:03:04.975
have double the level of stress hormones.

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.313
Which is why this
is the first skill to learn.

00:03:08.337 --> 00:03:10.284
You can't take care of them all at once,

00:03:10.308 --> 00:03:11.680
but you don't have to.

00:03:11.704 --> 00:03:12.926
Because we triage.

00:03:13.474 --> 00:03:15.823
Red -- immediately life-threatening.

00:03:15.847 --> 00:03:19.245
Yellow -- serious, but not
immediately life-threatening.

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:21.031
Green -- minor.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.610
And we focus our efforts
first on the reds.

00:03:25.245 --> 00:03:26.800
Now hear this.

00:03:27.149 --> 00:03:29.926
Part of the problem in Crazy Mode

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.547
is that you are reacting to everything

00:03:32.571 --> 00:03:34.769
as if it is red.

00:03:36.332 --> 00:03:40.037
So start by triaging correctly.

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:42.194
Know your reds.

00:03:42.616 --> 00:03:46.417
They're what is most important
and where you can most move the needle.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.179
Now it's easy to be confused by noise,

00:03:49.203 --> 00:03:52.932
but what it noisiest
is not always what is most red.

00:03:53.472 --> 00:03:59.551
In fact, my severe asthmatic patient
is most at risk when he's quiet.

00:04:00.212 --> 00:04:04.375
But my patient over here, demanding
that I bring her flavored coffee creamer,

00:04:04.399 --> 00:04:07.775
she's noisy, but she's not red.

00:04:08.736 --> 00:04:10.752
I'll give you an example from my own life.

00:04:10.776 --> 00:04:12.522
Last spring, my house flooded,

00:04:12.546 --> 00:04:14.522
my one-year-old was in the ER,

00:04:14.546 --> 00:04:17.601
I was supposed to do a fundraiser
for my four-year-old's school

00:04:17.625 --> 00:04:22.315
and the final chapter of my book
was beyond late.

00:04:23.682 --> 00:04:26.777
Maybe not ironically,
that was the chapter on stress.

00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:28.535
(Laughter)

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.222
My red tasks were getting
my one-year-old better

00:04:32.246 --> 00:04:34.172
and finishing my book.

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:35.370
That was it.

00:04:35.394 --> 00:04:38.579
Remember, relentlessly triage.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:40.466
The house flood repair?

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:44.252
Well, once we had stopped
and stabilized the damage,

00:04:44.276 --> 00:04:45.809
it was no longer a red.

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:47.791
It felt red,

00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:50.127
but it was in fact just noise.

00:04:50.543 --> 00:04:52.392
No, no really, it was quite noisy,

00:04:52.416 --> 00:04:55.196
this picture on the far right
is me wearing earplugs

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.646
to focus on my book,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.756
while the floor is being
mechanically dried around me.

00:05:00.458 --> 00:05:02.315
Know your reds,

00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:06.140
and do not let your non-reds
distract you from them.

00:05:06.974 --> 00:05:09.688
By the way, it is liberating
with a green task

00:05:09.712 --> 00:05:12.256
to, every once in a while,
be able to remind yourself,

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.459
"That's a green task.
No one's going to die."

00:05:15.483 --> 00:05:16.594
(Laughter)

00:05:16.618 --> 00:05:19.824
It's OK if it's not perfect.

00:05:22.118 --> 00:05:26.331
Now there's one last triage level
that we use in the worst scenarios.

00:05:26.355 --> 00:05:27.555
And that is black.

00:05:28.347 --> 00:05:32.160
Those patients for whom
there is nothing we can do.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:34.631
Where we must move on.

00:05:36.188 --> 00:05:38.125
And although it is gut-wrenching,

00:05:38.149 --> 00:05:39.299
I mention it,

00:05:39.323 --> 00:05:44.243
because you each have your own equivalent
black tasks in your life.

00:05:44.854 --> 00:05:48.390
These are items
that you must take off your list.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.233
And I think many of you
know what I'm talking about.

00:05:51.716 --> 00:05:54.176
For me, this was the fundraiser.

00:05:54.526 --> 00:05:55.882
I had to step down.

00:05:56.542 --> 00:05:58.476
Because as we in the ER know,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.736
if you try to do everything,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.426
you have no hope of saving your reds.

00:06:05.942 --> 00:06:09.053
Step two to go from Crazy Mode
into Ready Mode

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:12.664
is to expect and design for crazy.

00:06:13.188 --> 00:06:17.043
Half of handling crazy
is how you prepare for it.

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:19.321
So if step one we triage,

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:23.054
step two, we design to make
those tasks easier to do.

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:26.807
Science shows us
that the more options we have,

00:06:26.831 --> 00:06:28.569
then the longer each decision takes.

00:06:28.593 --> 00:06:32.141
And the more decisions we have to make,
the more exhausted our brain gets

00:06:32.165 --> 00:06:35.077
and the less it is capable
of making good decisions.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.006
Which is why this step two

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:40.767
is about finding ways to reduce
your daily decisions.

00:06:41.482 --> 00:06:45.395
Here are four easy examples
you can use in your daily lifestyle.

00:06:45.832 --> 00:06:46.999
Plan.

00:06:47.023 --> 00:06:49.467
Plan your entire week's meals
on the weekend,

00:06:49.491 --> 00:06:51.313
so that when it's Wednesday at 6pm

00:06:51.337 --> 00:06:54.451
and everyone's hangry
and requesting pizza,

00:06:54.475 --> 00:06:58.170
you have no decisions to make
to get a healthy meal on the table.

00:06:59.392 --> 00:07:00.550
Automate.

00:07:00.574 --> 00:07:04.503
Never leave anything to remember
that you could automate,

00:07:04.527 --> 00:07:08.963
whether it's scheduling it as recurring
or saved list, or recurrent purchases.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:10.820
Colocate.

00:07:11.122 --> 00:07:12.479
When it comes to exercise,

00:07:12.503 --> 00:07:16.250
store all the equipment that you need
for a certain activity together,

00:07:16.274 --> 00:07:17.971
charged and ready,

00:07:17.995 --> 00:07:20.153
so you don't spend energy looking for it.

00:07:20.558 --> 00:07:23.875
And decrease temptations,

00:07:23.899 --> 00:07:26.312
for anyone driven by sugar cravings.

00:07:26.336 --> 00:07:27.526
Anyone?

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.101
Say aye, go ahead.

00:07:29.125 --> 00:07:31.320
That itself is its own form of Crazy Mode

00:07:31.344 --> 00:07:34.172
and self-medication for Crazy Mode,

00:07:34.196 --> 00:07:36.545
but stop working your willpower.

00:07:37.077 --> 00:07:39.085
Design differently.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.934
If a food is out of immediate reach,

00:07:41.958 --> 00:07:44.948
such that you have to use
a stool to reach it,

00:07:44.972 --> 00:07:47.266
even when it's chocolate,

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:53.004
study participants ate 70 percent less
without thinking about it.

00:07:53.028 --> 00:07:54.178
I know.

00:07:54.202 --> 00:07:55.688
Let that sit for a second.

00:07:55.712 --> 00:07:57.252
(Laughter)

00:07:57.276 --> 00:08:02.193
Design to make the choices
you wish to make easier.

00:08:02.495 --> 00:08:06.574
Which bring us to the third step
to go from Crazy Mode to Ready Mode,

00:08:06.598 --> 00:08:09.965
and that is to get out of your head.

00:08:10.455 --> 00:08:11.621
Come with me.

00:08:11.645 --> 00:08:12.795
Different story.

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:16.191
I'm working in a small, satellite ER,

00:08:16.215 --> 00:08:18.281
when a woman comes in in labor.

00:08:18.754 --> 00:08:22.818
I realize that the cord
is wrapped not once

00:08:22.842 --> 00:08:25.651
but twice around the baby's neck.

00:08:26.842 --> 00:08:29.135
And I'm the only doctor.

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:32.039
I was scared.

00:08:32.643 --> 00:08:35.381
But I couldn't let it derail me.

00:08:36.207 --> 00:08:39.651
Because, you see, we all get nervous.

00:08:39.675 --> 00:08:41.548
We all get scared,

00:08:41.572 --> 00:08:45.325
but it's what you do next that matters.

00:08:46.022 --> 00:08:48.101
That first feeling isn't the problem.

00:08:48.125 --> 00:08:50.005
It can be an important sign.

00:08:50.411 --> 00:08:52.577
The problem comes
when we let it derail us.

00:08:52.601 --> 00:08:54.424
When that internal monologue starts

00:08:54.448 --> 00:08:57.283
and we catastrophize
and we start to get that tunnel vision.

00:08:57.307 --> 00:08:59.736
That's how you think
when you're in Crazy Mode,

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.734
and you cannot solve anything that way.

00:09:04.791 --> 00:09:06.760
Now I promise to come back to the story,

00:09:06.784 --> 00:09:09.815
but first, how do I get out
of my own head?

00:09:10.648 --> 00:09:12.601
There are many tactics that you may hear,

00:09:12.625 --> 00:09:18.386
but for me, I find it best in the moment
to actively put my focus on someone else.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:21.674
To deliberately make myself
see the person in front of me,

00:09:21.698 --> 00:09:23.507
see myself in the arena with them --

00:09:23.531 --> 00:09:25.618
what do they need, what do they fear

00:09:25.642 --> 00:09:28.134
and how can I help?

00:09:28.952 --> 00:09:32.372
This may sound like a whole lot
of warm and fuzzy to you,

00:09:32.396 --> 00:09:33.815
but it's not.

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:36.768
In fact, research shows
that when you prime your brain

00:09:36.792 --> 00:09:39.014
with what is, essentially, compassion,

00:09:39.038 --> 00:09:42.419
we disrupt that tunnel vision
and internal monologue.

00:09:42.443 --> 00:09:43.776
You widen your perception,

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:47.609
so your brain can actually take in
broader information,

00:09:47.633 --> 00:09:51.986
so you see more possibilities
and can make better decisions.

00:09:53.336 --> 00:09:54.486
Try it.

00:09:55.241 --> 00:09:58.820
Know that your internal
monologue can derail you.

00:09:59.955 --> 00:10:04.535
And realize that when you get out
of your own head,

00:10:04.559 --> 00:10:07.518
you get out of your own way.

00:10:09.503 --> 00:10:11.177
Now what happened to that baby?

00:10:12.156 --> 00:10:13.982
I focused not on my fear,

00:10:14.006 --> 00:10:17.672
but on the mother and the baby
and what they needed me to do.

00:10:18.355 --> 00:10:21.704
And got the cord off of the baby's neck,

00:10:21.728 --> 00:10:24.228
and a healthy screaming,
kicking baby arrived,

00:10:24.252 --> 00:10:26.561
just as the dad ran in
from the parking lot,

00:10:26.585 --> 00:10:28.478
"Hi, you have a son, I'm Dr. Darria.

00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:30.672
Congratulations,
you want to cut the cord?"

00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:32.077
(Laughter)

00:10:32.101 --> 00:10:33.744
And for a moment,

00:10:33.768 --> 00:10:36.307
the strong cries of a newborn

00:10:36.331 --> 00:10:41.751
drowned out the beeps and the sirens
that are the normal sounds of the ER.

00:10:42.942 --> 00:10:45.209
But there was also something else.

00:10:45.823 --> 00:10:49.093
Because when I walked back
out of that mother's room,

00:10:49.117 --> 00:10:53.958
I saw several of my other patients
hovering nearby.

00:10:54.941 --> 00:10:58.250
I suddenly realized
that despite their own problems

00:10:58.274 --> 00:11:00.353
that had brought them
to the emergency room,

00:11:00.377 --> 00:11:04.179
they had all come together
to root for this baby.

00:11:04.203 --> 00:11:07.000
And they now together shared in the joy.

00:11:08.215 --> 00:11:12.325
Because that is what happens
when you go from Crazy Mode to Ready Mode.

00:11:12.739 --> 00:11:14.135
Others notice.

00:11:14.159 --> 00:11:16.253
They want it too,
they just don't know how,

00:11:16.277 --> 00:11:18.358
they just need one example.

00:11:18.684 --> 00:11:21.215
Which could be you.

00:11:23.494 --> 00:11:25.128
Own the busy.

00:11:25.787 --> 00:11:29.088
But stop calling it crazy.

00:11:30.299 --> 00:11:33.164
You've always had that ability.

00:11:34.021 --> 00:11:35.886
But now ...

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:37.060
you're ready.

00:11:37.878 --> 00:11:39.029
Thank you.

00:11:39.053 --> 00:11:41.714
(Applause)

